# A2 joins natural_end, where "society" is glossed as all mankind, to magistrate_end, where "that society" means the members of the political community.
# I do not count A2 as a bridge. S4 marks both senses openly ("all of his society, that is, all mankind" against "the members of that society"), so the argument does not quietly slide between them.
# What A2 actually asserts is that the end narrows when the power passes to the magistrate. Each claim keeps its own sense in every statement that uses it.
# "Property" in S3 likely has Locke's broad sense, matching "lives, liberties, and possessions" in S4, so it does not change sense either.
